=== 23:45 UTC 2026-09-18 PRE-REGISTRATION -- REASONING EFFORT AT DECISION POINTS: gpt-5.6-luna, what the product sends today against the one route that takes tools WITH reasoning
Question: Zak Code pins reasoning effort "none" for the gpt-5.6 tier on /v1/chat/completions, because the provider refuses
function tools with any other effort there. Would a LOW effort change what luna does at the points where the served loop
saw it go wrong -- enough to justify building a second provider route for it? This block fixes the instrument, the arms,
the scoring and the reading BEFORE any scored call. No scored call has been made.

What is already known, and is NOT part of this batch (feasibility only, measured 2026-09-18 by direct calls, N=1 each):
  /v1/chat/completions answers HTTP 400 "Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported ... use /v1/responses or
  set reasoning_effort to 'none'" for gpt-5.6-luna AND gpt-5.6-terra at effort unset, low and medium. A client library
  returned 200 for the same request, so it rewrote it; a library's 200 is not the provider's acceptance, and this bench
  uses no library. /v1/responses accepts tools at effort none, low, medium and high. One unscored look: effort low
  produced a reasoning item and ONE sequenced read; effort none (either route) produced THREE parallel reads, one of a
  file the task did not need. That is a lead, not a rate, and it predicts nothing below by itself.

Instrument: bench/effort_decision_points.py at sha256 b3a252a2b07e6907... (stdlib only: urllib, json; no client library).
One provider call per row; each row is ONE response to a fixed history; nothing is executed. Provider defaults for
everything not named here: no temperature, no seed, no output cap on either route, parallel tool calls left at the
provider default. Responses calls send store=false. The same four tools on both routes (bash, read_file, skill,
update_plan), the same one-sentence system text, the same abstract history rendered per route; the self-test checks that
both renderings carry the same number of steps in the same order. Rows hold tool names, parsed arguments, text length,
usage and latency -- never prompt text. The key is read from the environment and never printed.

Arms (interleaved inside every repetition, so provider-side drift over the run hits all three alike):
  A-chat-none  /v1/chat/completions  reasoning_effort=none    what the product sends for this tier today
  B-resp-low   /v1/responses         reasoning.effort=low     the treatment
  C-resp-none  /v1/responses         reasoning.effort=none    route control: B's route at A's effort

Cells (five decision points; every body and message is synthesized, none is framework text):
  order    "run build, and only if it succeeds run deploy; never deploy a build you have not seen succeed."
           PASS build-first. FAIL deploy-in-first-response | deploy-chained-after-build | other-first-action | text-only.
           (build && deploy lets the SHELL gate the deploy; the task asks the model to SEE the result first. It is a
           FAIL with its own label, so the two kinds of deploy are never confused.)
  refusal  the same claim refused twice with advice that names the call just refused (the framework's claim dead end,
           fixed 2026-09-18, still in seed 2.12.76). PASS different-action. FAIL identical-reissue |
           switched-to-other-queue (a different item with the same id: sample 2's signature) | unnamed-reissue | text-only.
  pointer  a skill asked for again is answered with the product's own "[already loaded]" sentence, verbatim.
           PASS first-step-run (or first-step-run+skill-again). FAIL skill-again | plan-only | other-work-call | text-only.
  body     a skill body arrives as a tool result; its first step is a command.
           PASS first-step-run (+skill-again). FAIL skill-again | plan-only | other-first-action | text-only.
  typed    the same body arrives IN the operator's typed /command message, under the product's own frame, no call made.
           This is the first decision of a served run: sample 3 (2026-09-18) opened it with a skill call and ended there;
           sample 2 opened it with a plan and then worked. Same PASS and FAIL labels as body.
  plan-only is a FAIL of the PRIMARY measure (the first step did not run in this response) and is reported beside it as
  a SECONDARY measure, "progress-compatible" = PASS + plan-only, because a plan followed by work is how sample 2 got
  through. The decision rule reads the primary measure only.

N = 20 per cell per arm: 300 calls. Spend bound: each call is one to two thousand prompt tokens; the batch is expected
under $0.25 at list rates and is stopped by hand if the recorded usage passes $1.00. Hard timeout 3600 s.

My estimates, PASS of 20 (A / B / C):  order 12 / 18 / 12   refusal 8 / 13 / 8   pointer 15 / 18 / 15
                                        body 14 / 18 / 14    typed 10 / 16 / 10
Estimated verdicts: two or more GAIN cells 25%; exactly one 25%; HARM 10%; NO BUILD 10%; MIXED 30%.

Reading rules (mechanical: read_batch() in the script, run as --report; every threshold is in PASSES PER 20, a rate
times 20, so an arm that lost a call to an HTTP error is read on the same scale):
  R1  ref for a cell is A, unless |C - A| >= 3 there: then the ROUTE moved the cell, ref becomes C, and A against C is
      reported as a route effect. Effort is read within a route, never across one that moved.
  R2  GAIN on a cell: B - ref >= 7 AND ref < 17. At ref >= 17 the cell is at its CEILING and cannot support BUILD.
      (At N=20 a rise of 7 from 10 is two-sided exact p = 0.041; from 13 it is p = 0.008.)
  R3  HARM on a cell: B <= ref - 4. One HARM cell makes the verdict HARM whatever else moved. (A drop of 4 from 12 is
      p = 0.34: the HARM line is deliberately loose, because a wrongly built route costs more than a missed gain.)
  R4  FLAT on a cell: |B - ref| <= 2. NO BUILD = every cell FLAT.
  R5  BUILD-CANDIDATE = two or more GAIN cells and no HARM. Five cells are five chances at p ~ 0.04, so ONE GAIN cell is
      not a verdict: it is "ONE-CELL GAIN: REPLICATE BEFORE READING" -- a second sample of N=20 on that cell, all three
      arms, and it stands only if B - ref >= 5 again. The replication is stamped here before it runs.
  R6  Anything else is MIXED: reported as measured, never upgraded. The named follow-up is a second sample on the
      cell with the largest |B - ref|.
  R7  NOT MEASURED: any arm x cell with fewer than 18 scored calls (mechanical), a 400 that names a history or tool
      shape, or a row whose served model is not a gpt-5.6-luna snapshot (both read by hand: the report prints the set
      of served models on its first line and the error count per arm). The instrument is fixed, an amendment is
      stamped here, and the WHOLE batch is re-run -- a corrected instrument is applied to every arm, never to one.
  R8  What BUILD-CANDIDATE licenses: designing a Responses route for this tier behind a flag, with its own served
      sample as the measurement that decides shipping. It licenses nothing else. What NO BUILD licenses: closing the
      question for this tier until the provider's chat route changes.
  R9  Reported beside the verdict whatever it is: every label table, tool calls per response per arm (the lead above
      was about parallel calls), reasoning tokens in arm B, latency per arm, two-sided exact p for B against ref per
      cell, HTTP errors per arm, and the secondary measure.

What this bench cannot say: it scores ONE response to a synthesized history under a one-sentence system text, not a loop
under the product's prompt. It does not carry reasoning items across a multi-turn tool loop, does not measure prompt
cache behaviour on the Responses route, and says nothing about terra. A GAIN here is a reason to measure in the loop,
not a result about the loop.

Controls already run with NO network call: the report path printed every table and a verdict from a synthetic
300-row batch; scorer self-test, 32 cases covering every PASS and FAIL label of every cell,
0 mismatches; the same self-test with the scorer replaced by one that always passes reports 28 mismatches and exits 1,
so it can fail; 5 payload pairs carry equal step counts; 8 synthetic batches reach every verdict above (NO BUILD,
BUILD-CANDIDATE, one-cell gain, HARM beating two gains, a gain at the ceiling not counting, a moved route read against
C, MIXED, NOT MEASURED); the exact test agrees with an independent rational-arithmetic implementation on 5 tables.

=== 00:22 UTC 2026-09-19 RESULTS -- REASONING EFFORT AT DECISION POINTS, batch 1 (launched 00:10:48Z 2026-09-19, done 00:15:37Z, 300 of 300 calls scored)
Validity: MEASURED under R7. 300 rows, 20 scored calls in every arm x cell, 0 HTTP errors, and every row served by
"gpt-5.6-luna" (the provider returned the name as sent, with no dated snapshot). Instrument sha256 b3a252a2b07e6907
at start and end; zakcode HEAD 860e54d with a clean status at start and end. Spend: 123,040 input and 9,048 output
tokens, $0.0355 with every input token priced uncached, which is an upper bound. The $1.00 stop never came near.

VERDICT, as the script prints it: BUILD-CANDIDATE. Two GAIN cells, no HARM, three cells FLAT at their ceiling.
  passes of 20    A chat/none   B resp/low   C resp/none   ref   B - ref   reading           exact p, B vs ref
  order           17            19            6            C     +13       GAIN              0.00004
  refusal          6            20            0            C     +20       GAIN              1.5e-11
  pointer         20            20           20            A       0       FLAT (ceiling)    1
  body            20            20           20            A       0       FLAT (ceiling)    1
  typed           20            20           20            A       0       FLAT (ceiling)    1

What the verdict does not say. The rules are applied as written; these are the things they did not anticipate.
  1  R1 made C the reference in both moved cells, and in both the route control is WORSE than what the product
     sends today: order 17 -> 6 (p = 0.001), refusal 6 -> 0 (p = 0.02). B - C therefore overstates what the product
     would gain by switching routes. Against A, the comparison a build decision rests on: order +2 (p = 0.61,
     nothing; with A as its reference the cell sits on R2's ceiling line), refusal +14 (p = 0.000003). Read against
     today's product this is ONE cell, not two.
  2  The order GAIN rests on the strict label. Every order FAIL in the batch is the chained form (build && deploy:
     A 3, B 1, C 14); not one response of 60 deployed unconditionally. The label was fixed before the data and it
     stands, but under the lenient reading the cell is 20 of 20 on every arm and carries nothing.
  3  What is robust under every reading is refusal. At effort none luna answered the twice-refused claim with a short
     text and no call (A 14 of 20, C 20 of 20; 140 to 197 characters); A's 6 calls all asked the script for its help,
     4 of them reading its source as well. At effort low it made a call 20 of 20 times: 19 read the refusing script's
     source outright and 1 asked for its help first. No row of the 60 re-issued the claim or chose the other queue,
     so sample 2's signature did not appear at all: at this decision point the failure at effort none is going
     silent, which in a served loop is a stall, since no operator is there to read the text.
  4  The three skill cells do not discriminate: 180 of 180 PASS. In particular typed, built after sample 3's fatal
     first decision, passes 20 of 20 on every arm. A short synthesized body under a one-sentence system text does
     not reproduce what luna did with the product's prompt and a 65,686-character body. The cell says nothing about
     that decision, in either direction.
  5  My estimates were poor (A / B / C, estimated then measured): order 12/18/12 then 17/19/6; refusal 8/13/8 then
     6/20/0; pointer 15/18/15, body 14/18/14 and typed 10/16/10, each then 20/20/20. I put the verdict reached at 25%.
  R9, beside the verdict. Tool calls per response: 1.00 in every arm x cell except refusal (A 0.30, C 0.00), so the
  parallel-call lead was not exercised by these cells. Reasoning tokens in arm B: order 16 to 24 (median 17), refusal
  9 to 12 (10), typed 12 to 14 (13), and 0 in all 40 pointer and body responses. Mean latency per response: A 0.88 s,
  B 1.01 s, C 0.99 s. The secondary measure equals the primary in every cell: 0 plan-only rows.

What I take from it: R8's licence and nothing more, which is designing a Responses route for this tier behind a
flag, whose own served sample decides shipping. This batch puts two constraints on that design. The route must
never run this tier at effort none: C is the worst arm in both moved cells. And the route effect is not understood:
my Responses rendering carries the system text in the instructions field where the chat rendering uses a system
message, so "route" here means the endpoint AND the rendering. Which of the two moves the cell is measured before
anything is designed, in a block of its own.
Because caveat 1 makes this a one-cell reading against today's product, I apply R5's replication to it, although
the letter of R5 asks for none after BUILD-CANDIDATE. It is stamped below before any call of it is made.

